WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:07.360
Where does Batman get those wonderful toys? Is The&nbsp;
Riddler using up his cellphone minutes? And does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:12.000
Batman have a weird way of introducing himself&nbsp;
to criminals? All that and more, right here.
 &nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.440
In interviews describing his approach to&nbsp;
making Batman a more grounded character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.400
on the big screen, Matt Reeves may have&nbsp;
accidentally led fans to believe he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.640
eschewing some of the more fantastical elements of&nbsp;
the mythology in an effort to stick with realism.&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.280
"And I thought well one place we haven't been&nbsp;
is grounding it the way that Year One does."&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.800
But within the film's first few scenes,&nbsp;
we can see that isn't exactly the case.&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.480
When Bruce arrives home from a night of crime&nbsp;
fighting, he takes his contact lenses out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.320
reveal that they're actually extremely tiny&nbsp;
digital video cameras. He downloads data from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:56.080
them on a nightly basis to revisit the details&nbsp;
of his work when updating his ongoing diary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.280
and case files. Bruce repurposes this tech&nbsp;
later in the film when he needs Selina Kyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.600
to infiltrate 44 Below at the Iceberg Lounge.
Through Selina's eyes, Bruce is able to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.640
all the crooked cops and politicians who&nbsp;
regularly haunt this secret mob hangout.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.960
But when she and Bruce argue after Selina&nbsp;
runs off to chase a different lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.720
she angrily pries the camera lens from her eye and&nbsp;
tosses it into a sink, severing the connection.&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.320
However, much later in the film, when Selina needs&nbsp;
a way to communicate with Batman, suddenly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.680
discarded lens makes a reappearance. But nothing&nbsp;
gives the implication that Selina saved it for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.320
later, or that it's a piece of tech you can just&nbsp;
throw in a purse and keep safe for future use.&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:45.120
Speaking of mysterious gadgetry, "The Batman"&nbsp;
differs from Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:51.200
key way. There is no Lucius Fox to outfit Bruce&nbsp;
with all the wonderful toys he utilizes in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.840
war on crime.
"What's that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.480
Oh, the Tumbler? Oh, you&nbsp;
wouldn't be interested in that."&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.560
Some elements are self-explanatory. It's clear,&nbsp;
from the design alone, that Bruce has made the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.920
Batsuit himself, and some of the more rudimentary&nbsp;
tools he deploys are simple enough. We don't need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.600
to know where he got a grappling hook or a flare.
But the aforementioned digital video contacts?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.920
The glider armor he uses to escape the roof of&nbsp;
the GCPD building? Some of the computer tech in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.520
the Bat-cave? We have to essentially accept that,&nbsp;
just like other adaptations, all this stuff comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:35.680
from some unseen Wayne Enterprises R&amp;D division,&nbsp;
even if that answer is unsupported by the casual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.320
way Bruce insists he doesn't care about the&nbsp;company or anything that goes along with it. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.160
In one of the film's most tense moments,&nbsp;
D.A. Colson crashes the Mayor's funeral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.440
with his mouth duct-taped shut, a smartphone&nbsp;
affixed to his hand, and a bomb strapped around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.320
his neck. From the moment Colson arrives&nbsp;
with the bomb, the smartphone is ringing.&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.720
But a significant chunk of time passes between&nbsp;
Colson arriving and Batman answering the phone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.920
Day literally turns to night. Are we to believe&nbsp;
the Riddler was sitting in his murder lair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.520
mashing "redial" for hours on end, idly twirling&nbsp;
question marks into lattes to pass the time?&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.640
The cops probably wanted to&nbsp;
err on the side of caution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.880
and worried answering the phone would be&nbsp;
the detonator trigger for setting it off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.640
so it makes sense. But somewhere around the 42nd&nbsp;
ring, was the Riddler maybe second-guessing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.960
particular part of his larger plan? Or&nbsp;
did he foresee the hours he would spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:39.200
redialing the same number over and over?
"I've been trying to reach you."
 
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.760
One of the most interesting elements of&nbsp;
"The Batman" is how the Batsuit looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.800
like something Bruce could have conceivably&nbsp;
made himself. It feels less like a giant mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.400
of rubber molded into fake musculature we've&nbsp;
seen in past films. But there's one key element&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.440
of the plot that keeps recurring throughout and&nbsp;
makes it harder and harder to suspend disbelief.&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.960
In multiple scenes, Bruce is shown walking around&nbsp;
or riding his motorcycle in street clothes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:11.120
carrying a smallish backpack. The suggestion is&nbsp;
that he changes into the Batman suit later. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.320
save for one very quick series of shots early in&nbsp;
the film, we never see the process of what it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.000
like to put on the uniform. It appears far too&nbsp;
bulky and elaborate to fit into that backpack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.600
even though we're meant to surmise each&nbsp;
time that Bruce is ducking into alleyways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.000
and returning from the shadows enshrouded in&nbsp;
his war paint, ready to fight the good fight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.560
All that's missing is a Sailor&nbsp;
Moon transformation dance.
 &nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.000
"I'm vengeance."
This line in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.880
Batman" is first spoken during the character's&nbsp;
first onscreen appearance during a fight with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.160
gang of face-painted thugs. It returns as&nbsp;
a callback in several places in the film.&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.200
When Selina first meets Batman, the&nbsp;
Penguin refers to him as "Mr. Vengeance,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:01.520
leading Selina to keep using the nickname.
"Come on, Vengeance. Let's get in some trouble."&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.000
But… why did Penguin ever call Batman "Mr.&nbsp;
Vengeance" in the first place? He wasn't there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.720
when Bruce said that to the gang. Did Batman&nbsp;
ever say it to him? It feels like a fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.840
wall-breaking moment to something the audience&nbsp;
is privy to, but not the individual character.&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.080
Or, and this is somewhat weirder,&nbsp;
it implies that Bruce says this so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.000
often to criminals he beats up that it's&nbsp;
a running joke in the criminal community.&nbsp;

